.SH NAME
.PP
auto-add-group - Create a new user group

\" Convention:
\" Underline anything that is typed verbatim - commands, etc.
.SH SYNOPSIS
.PP
.nf 
.na
auto-add-group [-g GID] groupname
.ad
.fi

.SH ARGUMENTS
.nf
.na
-g GID      Force the new group to use GID
groupname   Name of the new group
.ad
.fi

.SH DESCRIPTION

The
.B auto-add-group
command creates a new user group.  The user can optionally specify
the numeric GID (group ID) using -g.  If the GID is not specified,
the next available GID is used.

On networks sharing files via NFS and other systems, it is
advisable to keep UIDs and GIDs the same across all systems,
to simplify file sharing.  Archiving software that stores UIDs
and GIDs rather than usernames and groupnames will also benefit.

NFSv4 can resolve different UIDs and GIDs, but this requires
additional configuration and running the nfsuserd/idmap service
on all systems.

.SH RETURN VALUES

0 on success, non-zero error codes otherwise.

.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
.na
auto-add-group
.ad
.fi

